1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,800
Hi! In this video, we're going to look at brute-forcing logins with Burp Suite.

2
00:00:10,800 --> 00:00:14,440
Although it's more efficient to first enumerate a valid username and then attempt to guess

3
00:00:14,440 --> 00:00:17,520
the matching password, this may not always be possible.

4
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:22,120
So in this video, we're going to look at using Burp Intruder to attempt to brute-force

5
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:26,240
both usernames and passwords in a single attack.

6
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:29,980
We're going to use a simplified example here to demonstrate how to use the relevant features

7
00:00:29,980 --> 00:00:31,460
of Burp Suite.

8
00:00:31,460 --> 00:00:35,960
To run this attack on real websites, you'll usually need to bypass defenses such as rate

9
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:37,440
limiting.

10
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:43,080
For ideas on how to do this, check out the authentication topic in the Web Security Academy.

11
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:46,520
Before you start, you'll need a list of potential usernames and passwords.

12
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:50,500
For this demo, we've included lists you can use.

13
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,340
In practice, we recommend sorting the list in order of how likely you think the username

14
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:56,820
or password is to be correct.

15
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:01,660
You can follow along with this demo using the Username Enumeration via Subtly Different

16
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:09,540
Responses Lab from our Web Security Academy.

17
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:14,300
Once the lab has launched, try to log in.

18
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:20,680
On the Proxy HTTP History tab, find the request for submitting the login form, right-click,

19
00:01:20,680 --> 00:01:25,620
and send it to Burp Intruder.

20
00:01:25,620 --> 00:01:34,780
Go to the Intruder tab, and on the Positions tab, set the attack type to ClusterBomb.

21
00:01:34,780 --> 00:01:40,120
In the request, highlight the username value, and click Add to mark it as a payload position.

22
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:46,100
Then do the same for the password.

23
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:50,040
Go to the Payload tab, and select Payload Set 1.

24
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:57,400
Make sure Payload Type is set to Simple List, and paste in the list of usernames.

25
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:14,280
Click Start Attack.

26
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:20,000
Once the attack has finished, study the responses for any behavior that may indicate valid credentials.

27
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:26,260
These could be a difference in error messages, different response times, or status codes.

28
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:31,100
In this example, we can see one of the responses has a 302 status code.

29
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:35,200
To investigate the contents of a response in detail, we can right-click and select Send

30
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:41,880
to Comparer Response, and also do the same for the original response.

31
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,880
Now when we go to the Comparer tab, we can select the two responses, and click Word or Bytes to

32
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:48,880
highlight any differences.

33
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:55,880
the first time, we can select the two responses.

